{
  "dom_id": "step-q39068",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "What is the index of the term $\\frac{79}{2}$ for the arithmetic sequence below?\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+1,\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,n\\ge 1\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We will convert the recursive formula to an explicit formula and then solve for the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=\\frac{79}{2}.$\n\nThe recursive rule for an arithmetic sequence takes the form\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+d.\n$$\n\nIn the given formula, we have $d=1.$ We are also told that ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2},$ so we can write the explicit formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(n-1)(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+n-1 \\\\  & =n-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ such that ${a}_{n}=\\frac{79}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =n-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{79}{2} & =n-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{79}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =n \\\\ 40 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{79}{2}$ is the $40$th term of the sequence.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
